Import target and the at line: $(<name>.import.target target,program) stores the program
as the variable target and enters the rules behind it, the cached value file and the
phony target of the name that prints it, as define.<name> did; its star form, given the
name of a defined variable, dedents its value and passes both. The one grammar amk adds is the at
line: an at sign and the name of a builtin with a star twin, alone at the start of a
line with optional arguments after it, applies that twin to the define that follows, as
if $(<builtin>* name,args) were written after its endef with its result discarded. A
line like that with no define after it is fatal; an at line that names no such builtin
is left to make as before. define.<name> is gone, and its rules are entered through the
same routine the builtin uses.

